        "original_id": "4765c54f-a696-5269-9a24-5c72dbcf000f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: whales, dolphins, porpoises, Cetacea, Cetartiodactyla\n- Animals: hippopotamuses, common hippopotamus, pygmy hippopotamus, Hippopotamidae\n- Places: sub-Saharan Africa\n\nFacts:\n- Whales are an informal grouping within the infraorder Cetacea and are usually defined to exclude dolphins and porpoises.\n- Whales, dolphins, and porpoises belong to the order Cetartiodactyla along with even-toed ungulates.\n- Whales, dolphins, and porpoises have hippopotamuses as their closest living relatives and diverged from them about 40 million years ago.\n- The common hippopotamus, Hippopotamus amphibius, is a large, mostly herbivorous, aggressive mammal that lives in sub-Saharan Africa.\n- Hippopotamidae has only two extant species: the common hippopotamus and the pygmy hippopotamus.\n- The pygmy hippopotamus is also known as Choeropsis liberiensis or Hexaprotodon liberiensis.",
